  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "there is/there are: affirmative descriptions with prepositions of place",
      "title_form": "There is / There are: descripciones afirmativas con preposiciones de lugar",
      "title_target": "There is / There are: affirmative descriptions with prepositions of place",
      "description_md_form": "Usamos **there is** para una cosa y **there are** para dos o más. Es la forma de decir *hay* en inglés.\n\n- **There is** + una cosa: `There is a bird in the tree.`\n- **There are** + dos o más cosas: `There are two cows on the farm.`\n\nPara decir *dónde* está algo, usamos preposiciones como **in** (en) y **near** (cerca de).\n\n| Singular | Plural |\n|----------|--------|\n| There is a river. | There are two rivers. |\n\nLa estructura es simple: **There is/are + cosa + lugar**. No cambia con la persona, solo con singular o plural.",
      "description_md_target": "We use **there is** for one thing and **there are** for two or more. It is the way to say *hay* in English.\n\n- **There is** + one thing: `There is a bird in the tree.`\n- **There are** + two or more things: `There are two cows on the farm.`\n\nTo say *where* something is, we use prepositions like **in** and **near**.\n\n| Singular | Plural |\n|----------|--------|\n| There is a river. | There are two rivers. |\n\nThe structure is simple: **There is/are + thing + place**. It does not change with the person, only with singular or plural.",
      "grammar_structure": "There is/are + noun + preposition + place",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "there_is_there_are",
        "headers": [
          "Forma",
          "Uso",
          "Ejemplo"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "There is",
            "Una cosa (singular)",
            "There is a mountain."
          ],
          [
            "There are",
            "Dos o más cosas (plural)",
            "There are three sheep."
          ]
        ]
      },
